  "narrative": "Independent validation closes the exact main-effect investigations for the three strongest dataset-specific predictors. H11 (KEAP1 loss versus intact) is accepted: the independent interval for the claimed higher-composite direction is +0.851 composite units with lower bound +0.756, comfortably above the 0.15-unit threshold and consistent with discovery. H9 (SMARCA4 loss versus intact) is accepted: the claimed lower-composite/stronger-dependency association validates as a signed +0.800 units, lower bound +0.706, matching discovery. H33 is also accepted: SMARCA4 loss versus intact among TP53-intact lines remains signed +0.795 with validation lower bound +0.709. This validates not only the SMARCA4 main effect but also a key co-alteration-restricted version of it, making the association less likely to be a simple proxy for measured TP53 loss. The central evidence set now supports three dataset-specific associations: KEAP1 loss associated with higher dependency_composite, reversed TP53 loss associated with higher dependency_composite, and SMARCA4 loss associated with lower dependency_composite, i.e. stronger measured dependency on this composite endpoint. These remain associations in the supplied lung dataset and do not establish causality, gene-specific knockout effects, or generalizability.\n\nSupported negative or null findings also remain important. Constructed research markers D/E/F and original directional hypotheses for KRAS, MTAP, ARID1A, STK11, MYC amplification, and hypoxia modification of KEAP1 did not support meaningful effects in the pre-registered directions, with intervals near zero or far below threshold. The original negative-direction TP53 hypothesis was decisively rejected by discovery and validation. Technical modification tests did not support meaningful batch_a concentration of KEAP1 or SMARCA4 associations, and did not support the pre-registered high-Cas9 positive KEAP1, negative SMARCA4, or positive TP53 interaction directions. The high-Cas9 interaction estimates point more toward possible attenuation, but the reversed attenuation directions would not be threshold-robust from the available intervals, so that interpretation remains cautious.\n\nUnresolved uncertainty is concentrated in boundary interactions and candidate secondary predictors. H50 remains unresolved: the KEAP1-by-TP53-loss interaction has point estimate +0.210 but lower bound only +0.019, so the data do not establish a meaningful nonadditive amplification. H74 remains unresolved: CDKN2A loss has a near-threshold signed estimate +0.141 with interval crossing the 0.15-unit boundary. The most informative next analyses are direct subgroup main effects rather than more difference-in-differences interactions: KEAP1 within TP53-loss lines, KEAP1/SMARCA4 within high-Cas9 lines, TP53 within high-Cas9 lines, and the new direct TP53-high-Cas9 proposal. Equally important are CDKN2A restrictions among TP53-intact, KEAP1-intact, and MTAP-intact lines to test whether the CDKN2A candidate survives exclusion of dominant co-alterations. Newly registered direct main effects by high serum and adenocarcinoma will help distinguish genuine subgroup magnitude differences from unstable interaction estimates. If CDKN2A restrictions or these direct subgroup effects become threshold-robust, independent validation should then be prioritized; otherwise these candidates should be closed or re-centered as exploratory dataset-specific findings.",
  "research_notes": "Current evidence snapshot: All three central accepted main effects are independently validated. KEAP1 loss versus intact: discovery +0.832 [0.766,0.897], validation +0.851 [0.756,0.945], accepted closed. SMARCA4 loss versus intact: discovery signed +0.833 [0.767,0.898], validation signed +0.800 [0.706,0.895], accepted closed. Reversed TP53 loss versus intact: discovery +0.423 [0.327,0.520], validation +0.421 [0.293,0.549], accepted closed. SMARCA4 loss in TP53-intact lines accepted from discovery and now independently validated (validation signed +0.795 [0.709,0.881]). Other robust accepted restricted SMARCA4 effects include ARID1A-intact, KEAP1-intact, and STK11-intact restrictions, though not all are independently validated. Other robust accepted TP53 restricted effects include KEAP1-intact, SMARCA4-intact, STK11-intact, and ARID1A-intact restrictions; other robust accepted KEAP1 restricted effects include STK11-intact, TP53-intact, and SMARCA4-intact restrictions.\n\nNegative/closed: research markers D/E/F main effects and adenocarcinoma marker-D interaction; original negative TP53 main effect; KRAS, MTAP, ARID1A, STK11, MYC amplification; KEAP1-hypoxia interaction; batch_a interactions for KEAP1 and SMARCA4; high-Cas9 pre-registered interaction directions for KEAP1, SMARCA4, TP53; SMARCA4 interaction by SMARCA4 co-alteration context? Specifically KEAP1 interaction in SMARCA4-loss and SMARCA4 interaction in TP53-loss rejected. Technical caveat: high-Cas9 interaction estimates suggest possible attenuation for KEAP1/SMARCA4 but not threshold-robust attenuation.\n\nBoundary unresolved: H50 KEAP1-by-TP53-loss interaction point estimate +0.210, lower +0.019. H74 CDKN2A main effect signed +0.141 [0.062,0.220], near threshold.\n\nRegistered pending/next priorities: direct KEAP1 main effect in TP53-loss lines (H79), direct KEAP1 main effect in high-Cas9 lines (H80), direct SMARCA4 main effect in high-Cas9 lines (H81), CDKN2A restrictions in TP53-intact/KEAP1-intact/MTAP-intact lines (H82-H84), and newly registered direct TP53 main effect in high-Cas9 lines plus direct main effects of KEAP1/SMARCA4/TP53 within high serum and adenocarcinoma. Also pending broad genomic screens: EGFR/ERBB2 amplifications, RB1, PTEN, BRAF, PIK3CA, NF1, BRCA2; technical/culture screens: batch, library, omics availability, serum contrasts; biological-state interactions: EMT/stemness/research signatures. Next analyze should prioritize direct subgroup main effects for accepted predictors and CDKN2A restrictions. Next validation should be considered only after a boundary candidate becomes threshold-robust, preferably CDKN2A or a stable subgroup-specific main effect, rather than spending validation on already validated central effects."
